# Promotion Checklist

**Scaffolded by**: `/scaffold-environments`
**Source methodology**: `.docs/policies/environment-promotion-policy.md`
**Status**: yours to maintain. Nothing in this file is enforced, and no hook,
command, or script in LogicLoom reads it.

---

## What this file is

The portable patterns from the environment-promotion methodology, written out
against **this repository's actual topology** so they can be used as a working
checklist rather than read as theory.

It was generated from the branches this repository had when the scaffolder ran:

| Role | Branch |
|---|---|
| production | `__PROD_BRANCH__` |
| integration | `__INTEGRATION_BRANCH_DISPLAY__` |
| staging / rehearsal | `__STAGING_BRANCH_DISPLAY__` |

Declared environments: `__ENV_NAMES_DISPLAY__`
(see `.logic-loom/config/environments.conf`; validate with
`./.logic-loom/scripts/bash/validate-environments.sh`)

**No branch was created.** The scaffolder only ever reads branches; if a role
above says *none*, that role has no branch in this repository and none was
invented for it.

---

## What LogicLoom did NOT scaffold, and will not

No deployment machinery of any kind: no pipeline, no cloud or CI-provider deploy
logic, no migration runner, no seed script, no teardown job, no rollback
mechanism, and no secret values. Those are the product's — this file is the
checklist against which you build them, and the `deploy` seam in
`environments.conf` is where you point at them.

---

## 1. The branch boundary — is it in CI, or only in convention?

A stated branching convention is not a boundary. It is a boundary when a check
**hard-fails** a pull request that violates it.

- [ ] A CI check fails any PR into `__PROD_BRANCH__` whose head is not an
      allowed branch. __CI_GUARD_STATUS__
- [ ] It **fails closed**: an empty or unreadable head ref is a failure, not a
      pass, and it prints why.
- [ ] It has **no exemptions** — no label, no actor allowlist, no skip flag.
      Widening the allowed set is an edit to a reviewed file; an escape hatch is
      not.
- [ ] It lives **on the base branch**. For `pull_request` events the workflow is
      evaluated from the base, so a PR cannot modify the copy that judges it —
      but only if that copy is merged to `__PROD_BRANCH__`.

---

## 2. The default-branch trap

Tooling that bases a new branch or worktree on "the default branch" reads a
pointer written once at clone time. It goes stale silently; nothing errors; the
wrong base is simply used.

**This repository's arrangement**: __TRAP_NARRATIVE__

- [ ] Every tool that creates a branch or worktree here resolves its base the
      way described above — and you have checked which, rather than assumed.
- [ ] __TRAP_ACTION__
- [ ] If the topology changes, this checklist and
      `__BRANCH_BASE_CHECK_DISPLAY__` are regenerated. They encode the
      arrangement that was true when they were written.

---

## 3. Migrate before deploy — structurally

- [ ] The migration step **gates** every deploy step that depends on it, as a
      dependency edge in the CI graph.
- [ ] Not as step ordering inside one job. Not as documentation. A documented
      ordering is followed until someone reorders steps in a hurry; a dependency
      edge cannot be skipped without editing the graph, which is reviewable.
- [ ] Every migration is **safely re-runnable** (Principle IV), and no migration
      is ever applied out-of-band. Both known failure modes in this methodology
      trace to out-of-band application desynchronizing what environments believe
      they have already applied.
- [ ] You have decided and **written down** whether migrations are forward-only
      or reversible. Both are legitimate; only the undecided case is a bug — and
      if you pick forward-only, say so wherever your rollback procedure is
      documented, or that section describes a capability you do not have.

---

## 4. Fail closed, with a typed reason

The single most reusable pattern here, and house style for every gate.

> A gate that cannot evaluate its own condition — an API outage, an unparseable
> date, an ambiguous ancestry check, an empty input — must **fail**, and must
> print (a) *why* it could not evaluate, and (b) *which* override exists, if one
> does. Never silently pass. Never silently fail.

- [ ] Every gate in the promotion path prints a reason on failure.
- [ ] No gate passes optimistically when its input is missing or unreadable.
- [ ] Where a gate is deliberately fail-**open** (a viewer, a report, anything
      that must not block a workflow), that is stated in the script itself, and
      any fail-closed companion lives in a **separate** script rather than as a
      flag on the fail-open one.

---

## 5. Confirmation scaled to blast radius

| Promoting into | Confirmation | Skippable? |
|---|---|---|
| dev-class environment | none, or a light prompt | yes |
| staging / rehearsal | a plain yes/no prompt | yes, via an automation flag |
| **production** | a **typed exact phrase** | **no — no flag bypasses it** |

- [ ] Each environment's `confirm` value in `environments.conf` matches what the
      promotion script actually does. The harness prompts for nothing; the
      declaration is a statement of intent you implement.
- [ ] The automation flag that suppresses ordinary prompts explicitly does
      **not** bypass the production confirmation.
- [ ] Where an override is genuinely needed at production scale, it is scoped to
      one specific **check** (a staleness bound, say) — never to the
      confirmation step.

